>
>/ -> "Move cursor to object to be identified, or hit / to identify a character"
>llljjj. -> "That's a cockatrice corpse."

Even easier, why not simply get rid of the '.' to pick an object? I.e. every
time you move over a symbol it is identified for you UNTIL you hit '.' and
then it finishes (wherever you are.)  

Let's face it, this command is used a great deal.  So, why not make it really
good? How about (using the above grammar):

/ --> "Description mode (h for help)"
/ h -->"Help:
	type [HJKLYUBNhjklyubn] for manual search 
        type m[A-z]+ for monster description
    	type [?!"$%^*()_]~] to examine each object of that type
	type M to examine each monster
	------------------------------"
/ .* [HJKLYUBN] :== jump to the middle of the nearest room in that direction
/ .* [hjklyubn] :== move in a direction
/ .* m[A-z]+ :== identify the monster whose name you type
/ .* [?]+ :== skip through all visible scrolls on levl one 
		at a time (**without** using --more--)
/ .* [!]+ :== skip through all visible potions on level 
/ .* ["]+ :== skip through all amulets/spider webs on level
	.
	.
	.
/ .* [M]+ :== skip through all monsters

/ .* <ESC> (or a literal .)

This identify can do multiples really easily by virtue of the fact that
you don't have to end the search until you get a . or an <esc> Therefore,
you can skip over to the next room (via 'H' or something) and then poke
around there.  You can step through the monster list and then look around
the room a monster is in and then continue looking through the monster list.
As _soon as_ you put the cursor onto an object, it is identified for you.

What do you think, oh critical world?

